<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pembrokeshire</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pembrokeshire is a county in the south-west corner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, with its administrative centre at Haverfordwest and the cathedral city of St <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">Davids</a>. Its southern part, settled by English and Flemish colonists under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a>, became known as 'Little <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> beyond Wales'. Its coastline forms the Pembrokeshire Coast National Park, created in 1952.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pembrokeshire.jpg" alt="Pembrokeshire" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dylan Moore · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tenby_Harbour_-_geograph.org.uk_-_3983172.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Pembrokeshire is bordered by Ceredigion to the north-east, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carmarthenshire">Carmarthenshire</a> to the east and otherwise by the sea. Its coast, formed by the complex geology of the area, includes high sea cliffs, wide sandy beaches, the ria of Milford Haven and offshore islands such as Ramsey, Skomer and Caldey, which are home to seabird colonies. The interior is relatively flat and gently undulating, except for the Preseli Mountains in the north. Most of the coast is protected by the Pembrokeshire Coast National Park, the only park in the United Kingdom established primarily because of its coastline, and it can be walked on the Pembrokeshire Coast Path.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Human habitation dates back between 125,000 and 70,000 years, and there are many prehistoric sites, particularly in the Preseli Mountains, including Pentre Ifan and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> remains. Roman influence was limited, with a few forts, coins and roads, and much of the area stayed beyond sustained Roman control. In the sub-Roman period the Irish Déisi settled and merged with local populations to form the Kingdom of Dyfed, which became part of Deheubarth in the 10th century. The region saw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raids and limited settlement.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> built several castles, such as Pembroke and Cilgerran, and St <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>'s Cathedral became an important pilgrimage site. Until the 12th century a great extent of the county was virgin woodland, and clearance in the lowland south began under Anglo-Flemish colonisation. Because of immigration, including the influx of Flemish people, the south has fewer Welsh speakers than the north. The rough boundary between the two regions is the Landsker Line, and the south has been called Little England beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>; George Owen of Henllys remarked on the distinction as early as 1603.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution">Industrial Revolution</a> the county stayed relatively rural, except for Milford Haven, which was developed as a port and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> dockyard. Slate quarrying was a significant industry in the 19th and early 20th centuries, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> was mined between the 14th and 20th centuries, the last mine closing at Kilgetty in 1950. Since the 1950s petrochemical and liquefied natural gas industries have developed along the Milford Haven Waterway. The county town is Haverfordwest, and St <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">Davids</a> is the smallest city in the United Kingdom. Under the Local Government (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>) Act 1994 Pembrokeshire became a unitary authority in 1996, after the abolition of Dyfed. It has more than 1,600 listed buildings.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pembrokeshire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>English and Flemish settlers occupied south <b>Pembrokeshire</b>, creating the 'Little England beyond Wales'.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-palatine-of-pembroke">County Palatine of Pembroke</a></li><li><blockquote>The Demetae lived in what is now <b>Pembrokeshire</b> and western Carmarthenshire, a region of small defended farmsteads, known as raths, and coastal promontory forts.</blockquote><a href="/state/demetae">Demetae</a></li></ul></section>
